{# agentgateway config-as-code — rendered by deploy-agentgateway.yml from
   inventory + OpenBao. Schema: https://agentgateway.dev/schema/config (v1.5.0).

   No credential is written here:
   - the upstream key is the env reference $VLLM_API_KEY (the gateway expands it
     from its environment; the value lives in the 0600 .env)
   - every client key is enrolled as `keyHash: sha256:<hex>` of the OpenBao
     value, so a reader of this file cannot use it to call the gateway — except
     under the local-dev-only `agw_plaintext_keys` flag, which trades that for a
     playground with saved keys
   Identity = the key's `metadata.name`, surfaced to CEL as `apiKey.name`
   (metadata is flattened onto the apiKey object — upstream
   crates/agentgateway/src/http/apikey.rs `Claims`): the identity label on metrics
   and logs. Per-identity limits are the token budgets attached to each key; the
   request bucket is one global ceiling (v1.5.0 has no per-identity request bucket
   under `llm.policies`).

   Deliberately absent (design §3): `retry` (a retried partial stream repeats tool
   side effects) and `timeout.requestTimeout` / `backendRequestTimeout` (streams
   run past any fixed value; the upstream keep-alive owns idle).
   The key loop reads `agw_identities`: inventory agw_clients plus one personal identity per
   declared platform_admins entry (vars/secret-declarations/agentgateway.yml). The fallback to
   agw_clients is for a caller that renders this file without that vars file; the deploy checks
   the render against agw_identities (deploy-agentgateway.yml).
   Do NOT edit by hand — re-run the playbook. #}
{# agw_ui_enabled (default true): false renders no UI listener and no OIDC block,
   so /v1 can be deployed and proven before the Authentik client exists. The
   admin listener still carries its own unauthenticated UI copy on the container
   loopback, which is never published. #}
{% set _ui = agw_ui_enabled | default(true) | bool %}
{# agw_mcp_enabled (default false; agentgateway-mcp G4): serves MCP at /mcp on the `default` gateway and adds the
   loopback gateway the MCP target calls. false renders none of it: the file is what it was before MCP. The loopback
   listener port (agw_loopback_port, default 4002) is also where the one target kind rendered here, an OpenAPI
   target over this gateway's own /v1, is aimed; the host is never an input, so no declaration can aim a target at
   the external listener or at /mcp (a target pointing back at /mcp recurses without a bound, spike 1.5). #}
{% set _mcp = agw_mcp_enabled | default(false) | bool %}
{% set _lb_port = agw_loopback_port | default(4002) %}
{# agw_listener_tls (default false; production-internal-ca task 5.2, gateway task 6.1): both
   listeners serve HTTPS from the step-ca-issued server leaf in the mounted /certs directory
   (`current/` is swapped by renewal; the image has no shell) and require a client certificate
   from the internal CA (`root`). `root` alone admits any leaf the CA signs, so each gateway also
   carries a `require` rule over the client certificate's SANs: the client-profile leaves named in
   agw_client_cert_allowlist (default caddy alone), resolved to their declared SANs in site-config
   `internal_leaves`. Measured on v1.5.0 (2026-10-02): no client certificate fails the handshake; a
   certificate outside the list is 403 even with a valid API key, because the rule runs before key
   authentication; an allowlisted one then needs its key (401 without). deploy-agentgateway.yml
   refuses an allowlist entry that names no declared client leaf, before any restart. #}
{% set _tls = agw_listener_tls | default(false) | bool %}
{% set _allow_sans = internal_leaves | default([]) | selectattr('profile', 'equalto', 'client')
     | selectattr('name', 'in', agw_client_cert_allowlist | default(['caddy']))
     | map(attribute='sans') | flatten | unique | sort %}
{# OpenRouter models (agentgateway-openrouter): an agw_models entry with `provider: openrouter` is
   served by the gateway's native OpenRouter provider; every other entry renders exactly as before.
   OpenRouter bills per token, so it is denied to every identity not named in
   agw_openrouter_identities or, with agw_openrouter_admins true, derived from platform_admins
   (_agw_admin_identities, from the vars file; absent there, only the explicit list counts): each key entry's allowedModels below drops the OpenRouter names for
   it (starting from its agw_client_policies allow-list, else every declared model). With no
   OpenRouter model declared, `_or_names` is empty and this changes nothing. Computed here from
   agw_models, not read from the vars file, so a caller that renders without that file is denied too
   instead of silently allowed. The deploy refuses an empty union; an undeclared one renders as
   "nobody". The two identities this file renders for itself, `mcp-self` and `legacy-shared`, are
   never in the union whatever the inventory says (the deploy refuses naming them too). #}
{% set _or_names = agw_models | selectattr('provider', 'defined') | selectattr('provider', 'equalto', 'openrouter')
     | map(attribute='name') | list %}
{% set _or_ids = ((agw_openrouter_identities | default([]))
     + ((_agw_admin_identities | default([]) | map(attribute='name') | list)
        if (agw_openrouter_admins | default(false) | bool) else []))
     | reject('in', ['mcp-self', 'legacy-shared']) | list %}
{% set _all_names = agw_models | map(attribute='name') | list %}
{# Budget tiers: agw_tier_tokens_per_hour maps a tier name (admin, developer, business) to an hourly
   token figure. An identity derived from platform_admins is tier `admin`; any other identity joins a
   tier with agw_client_policies.<name>.tier. An identity's budget is its own tokens_per_hour, else its
   tier's figure, else agw_rate_tokens_per_hour. Undeclared, no tier has a figure and every budget is
   what it was before. The deploy refuses a tier name absent from the map (_agw_tier_problems). #}
{% set _tier_budgets = agw_tier_tokens_per_hour | default({}) %}
{% set _admin_ids = _agw_admin_identities | default([]) | map(attribute='name') | list %}
{# Shared model pools: an agw_models entry with `pool_identity: <name>` gets an hourly token cap shared by
   every caller, because v1.5.0 budgets attach to API keys only, never to a model (crates/agentgateway/src/
   http/budget/mod.rs, `Budget`: name, limit, window, action). The entry renders twice:
   - its public name, served by a `custom` provider pointed at this gateway's own loopback listener under the
     pool identity's key (an env reference), asking for `pool/<name>`;
   - `pool/<name>`, the declared upstream, allowed to the pool identity ALONE.
   So every call is charged twice: to the caller's own budget, and to the pool identity's, whose
   agw_client_policies.<pool>.tokens_per_hour is the shared cap. Every other key entry has the `pool/` names
   removed from its allowedModels, the legacy shared key included, so no key can skip the pool by asking for
   the hidden name. The pool identity is allowed only `pool/` names, so a public-name call that lands on the
   loopback cannot forward to itself. Measured on the v1.5.0 image 2026-10-26: the pool's 429 reaches the
   caller as a 429, a direct call to `pool/<name>` with another key is 403, and the caller's own budget is
   charged from the relayed usage. The deploy refuses a pool it cannot render safely (_agw_pool_problems). #}
{% set _pool_prefix = 'pool/' %}
{% set _pooled = agw_models | selectattr('pool_identity', 'defined') | list %}
{% set _pool_names = _pooled | map(attribute='name') | map('regex_replace', '^', _pool_prefix) | list %}
{% set _pool_ids = _pooled | map(attribute='pool_identity') | unique | list %}
{# The loopback listener serves the MCP target's self-call and the pools' self-call alike. #}
{% set _loopback = _mcp or (_pooled | length > 0) %}
{% macro listener_tls() %}
    tls:
      cert: /certs/{{ agw_server_leaf | default('agw-server') }}/current/cert.pem
      key: /certs/{{ agw_server_leaf | default('agw-server') }}/current/key.pem
      root: /certs/step-ca-bundle.crt
    authorization:
      rules:
        - require: 'source.subjectAltNames.exists(n, n in {{ _allow_sans | to_json | replace("'", "") }})'
{% endmacro %}
{# The one apiKey policy, for every surface that authenticates clients: `llm` and `mcp` both call this, so
   the key loop is written ONCE and the two lists cannot drift. identities = the names to enrol; legacy = whether
   the shared-key grace-period entry follows them; limits = whether each entry carries its model allow-list and token
   budget. The llm policy takes all three. The mcp policy takes none: a budget under mcp.policies.apiKey is accepted
   but never charged or enforced (spike 1.4), a silent no-op that reads as a control, and the legacy shared key and the
   internal MCP self-call identity are never admitted there (design G2). Emits the whole `apiKey:` block at policy
   indent, ending with a newline: call it with `| trim('\n')` so the template's own newline closes the last line. #}
{% set _internal_ids = ['mcp-self'] + _pool_ids %}
{% macro apikey_policy(identities, legacy, limits) %}
    apiKey:
      # strict: a request with no key or an unknown key is 401 here and never
      # reaches the upstream (spec scenario "Unknown key is rejected").
      mode: strict
      location:
        header:
          name: authorization
          prefix: 'Bearer '
      keys:
{% for c in identities %}
{% set _pol = (agw_client_policies | default({}))[c] | default({}) %}
{% if (agw_plaintext_keys | default(false) | bool) and (local_mode | default(false) | bool) and c not in _internal_ids %}
      # LOCAL-DEV ONLY (inventory agw_plaintext_keys: true): the value itself, so the
      # UI's playground can offer it as a saved key. Prod keeps hashes — the rendered
      # config is then never a credential.
      - key: {{ secrets['client_' ~ c] }}
{% else %}
      - keyHash: sha256:{{ secrets['client_' ~ c] | hash('sha256') }}
{% endif %}
        metadata:
          name: {{ c }}
{% if limits %}
{% set _has_am = _pol.allowed_models is defined %}
{% set _am = _pol.allowed_models | default([]) %}
{% if _or_names and c not in _or_ids %}
{% set _has_am = true %}
{% set _am = (_am if _pol.allowed_models is defined else _all_names) | reject('in', _or_names) | list %}
{% endif %}
{% if c in _pool_ids %}
{% set _has_am = true %}
{% set _am = _pooled | selectattr('pool_identity', 'equalto', c) | map(attribute='name') | map('regex_replace', '^', _pool_prefix) | list %}
{% elif _pool_names %}
{% set _am = (_am if _has_am else _all_names) | reject('in', _pool_names) | list %}
{% set _has_am = true %}
{% endif %}
{% if _has_am %}
        # Per-identity model allow-list (inventory agw_client_policies.<name>.allowed_models).
        allowedModels: {{ _am | to_json }}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if limits %}
        # Per-identity token budget, BEST-EFFORT: a rolling UTC-hour window charged
        # AFTER each response from the upstream's reported usage (upstream docs,
        # cost-controls/budget-limits: the request that crosses the limit completes,
        # the NEXT one is rejected, and a response without usage is not charged at
        # all). Each model's `transformation` below makes every streamed chat request
        # carry usage on every chunk, so a stream is charged even when the client opts
        # out or disconnects early (task 2.3a). It is a spend guard, not a
        # hard cap; the hard admission control is the global request bucket below.
        # Default figure from inventory (design §4), then the identity's tier
        # figure, then its own agw_client_policies.<name>.tokens_per_hour. The only
        # per-identity control v1.5.0 offers, and the reason the db exists.
{% set _tier = _pol.tier | default('admin' if c in _admin_ids else '') %}
        budgets:
        - name: hourly-tokens
          limit:
            unit: Tokens
            amount: {{ _pol.tokens_per_hour | default(_tier_budgets[_tier] if _tier in _tier_budgets else (agw_rate_tokens_per_hour | default(2000000))) }}
          window:
            rolling: 1h
          onBudgetExceeded: Block
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if legacy and (_legacy_shared_active | default(false) | bool) %}
{% set _lp = (agw_client_policies | default({}))['legacy-shared'] | default({}) %}
      # Shared-key grace period (gateway task 5.1): the key every client used before the
      # gateway, enrolled as ONE identity until inventory legacy_shared_expires. Its hash is the
      # fingerprint recorded in OpenBao (pinned to the pre-rotation key), or, before the first
      # recording, the current vLLM key's. On or after the date this block is not rendered.
      - keyHash: sha256:{{ _legacy_fp_recorded | default('', true) or (secrets.vllm_api_key | hash('sha256')) }}
        metadata:
          name: legacy-shared
{% set _has_am = _lp.allowed_models is defined %}
{% set _am = _lp.allowed_models | default([]) %}
{% if _or_names and 'legacy-shared' not in _or_ids %}
{% set _has_am = true %}
{% set _am = (_am if _lp.allowed_models is defined else _all_names) | reject('in', _or_names) | list %}
{% endif %}
{% if _pool_names %}
{% set _am = (_am if _has_am else _all_names) | reject('in', _pool_names) | list %}
{% set _has_am = true %}
{% endif %}
{% if _has_am %}
        allowedModels: {{ _am | to_json }}
{% endif %}
        budgets:
        - name: hourly-tokens
          limit:
            unit: Tokens
            amount: {{ _lp.tokens_per_hour | default(agw_rate_tokens_per_hour | default(2000000)) }}
          window:
            rolling: 1h
          onBudgetExceeded: Block
{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://agentgateway.dev/schema/config
config:
  # Bind on all container interfaces; compose publishes them on the host binds
  # from .env (loopback by default). The admin listener (config dump, unauthenticated
  # UI copy) is pinned to the container loopback explicitly rather than trusted to
  # the upstream default, and is never published.
  adminAddr: 127.0.0.1:15000
  readinessAddr: 0.0.0.0:19001
  statsAddr: 0.0.0.0:19002
  # The gateway's own Postgres, for per-API-key token budgets only (v1.5.0
  # refuses budgets without config.database). The URL carries the password, so
  # it is an env reference expanded by the gateway, never written here.
  database:
    url: $AGW_DATABASE_URL
  # Observability (upstream llm/observability): per-identity request and token
  # series on the stats endpoint. `apiKey.name` is bounded by the enrolled
  # identities, so the label cardinality is small and known.
  metrics:
    fields:
      add:
        identity: apiKey.name
# The built-in operator UI, on its OWN listener (:4001) instead of the admin
# interface (which stays on the container loopback, unpublished), and
# authenticated BY THE GATEWAY: an OIDC authorization-code flow against Authentik
# in ui.policies (the documented "Secure the UI" path — the UI only recognises
# its own policies; a Caddy forward_auth gate in front left it warning "UI is
# exposed without authentication", 2026-09-17). Caddy is a plain TLS proxy.
# Verified on v1.5.0: `/` -> 308 `/ui`, assets under /ui/assets, API under /ui/api;
# discovery + token exchange trust the IdP via SSL_CERT_FILE (rustls-native-certs).
gateways:
  default:
    port: 4000
{% if _tls %}
{{ listener_tls() }}
{%- endif %}
{% if _loopback %}
  # The MCP target's own call to /v1 (agentgateway-mcp task 3.7), and a pooled model's call to its hidden
  # `pool/<name>` entry (the shared model pools above): plain HTTP, attached to `llm` only,
  # never published. v1.5.0 gateways have no bind address, so this listener is on every container
  # interface; the rule below is what keeps it to the gateway process's own 127.0.0.1 (a neighbour
  # container is 403 with or without a key, spike 1.5). It needs no client certificate, so the
  # external listener's SAN allowlist is not widened for the self-call.
  loopback:
    port: {{ _lb_port }}
    authorization:
      rules:
        - require: 'source.address == "127.0.0.1"'
{% endif %}
{% if _ui %}
  ui:
    port: 4001
{% if _tls %}
{{ listener_tls() }}
{%- endif %}

ui:
  gateways: ui
  policies:
    oidc:
      issuer: {{ agw_oidc_issuer | default('https://auth.agent-cloud.test:8443/application/o/agentgateway/') }}
      clientId: agentgateway
      # Env reference (OIDC_* values live in the 0600 .env); the same secret the
      # Authentik blueprint injects, shared-read from secret/services/authentik.
      clientSecret: $AGW_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET
      redirectURI: {{ agw_ui_redirect_uri | default('https://admin.inference.agent-cloud.test:8443/oidc/callback') }}
      scopes: [profile, email]
    # Admin tier, enforced here too: the ID token's `groups` claim (Authentik's
    # profile scope) must carry the platform admin group.
    authorization:
      rules:
        - allow: '"{{ agw_ui_admin_group | default('platform-admins') }}" in jwt.groups'
{% endif %}

frontendPolicies:
  # Access logs carry model, usage, and streaming timings by default. Add the
  # bounded client identity without recording prompts or completions. The old
  # config.logging setting cannot coexist with this policy (v1.5.0).
  accessLog:
    add:
      identity: apiKey.name
      # v1.5.0 emits built-in http.path (telemetry/log.rs:1516). Its same-name
      # add overrides the default: LoggingFields::has checks additions, then each
      # stdout/OTLP emitter clears that key before appending CEL results
      # (log.rs:429-430, 1822-1834, 1842-1856). Default request fields include
      # http.host and http.path; mcp.resource.uri is a distinct resource URI
      # (log.rs:1514-1516, 1579-1583), not a full request URL.
      # CEL RequestRef distinguishes query-free path from URI/path-and-query
      # (types.rs:905-910); its mapping uses URI.path() for path (types.rs:1104-1109).
      # Pinned v1.5.0 sources:
      # https://github.com/agentgateway/agentgateway/blob/v1.5.0/crates/agentgateway/src/telemetry/log.rs#L429-L430
      # https://github.com/agentgateway/agentgateway/blob/v1.5.0/crates/agentgateway/src/telemetry/log.rs#L1514-L1516
      # https://github.com/agentgateway/agentgateway/blob/v1.5.0/crates/agentgateway/src/telemetry/log.rs#L1579-L1583
      # https://github.com/agentgateway/agentgateway/blob/v1.5.0/crates/agentgateway/src/telemetry/log.rs#L1822-L1834
      # https://github.com/agentgateway/agentgateway/blob/v1.5.0/crates/agentgateway/src/telemetry/log.rs#L1842-L1856
      # https://github.com/agentgateway/agentgateway/blob/v1.5.0/crates/agentgateway/src/cel/types.rs#L905-L910
      # https://github.com/agentgateway/agentgateway/blob/v1.5.0/crates/agentgateway/src/cel/types.rs#L1104-L1109
      http.path: request.path
{% if agw_otlp_host | default('') and agw_otlp_logs | default(false) | bool %}
    # Access logs share Alloy's OTLP receiver with traces. The service field
    # becomes Loki's bounded service label; stdout logging stays available.
    otlp:
      host: {{ agw_otlp_host }}
      fields:
        add:
          service: '"agentgateway"'
          identity: apiKey.name
          signal: '"access-log"'
          # OTLP has its own field overrides; keep the same-name replacement explicit.
          http.path: request.path
{% endif %}
{% if agw_otlp_host | default('') and agw_otlp_traces | default(false) | bool %}
  tracing:
    # The receiver is Alloy, which batches these spans to Tempo. Bound both
    # newly started and client-propagated traces; no prompt/completion fields.
    host: {{ agw_otlp_host }}
    randomSampling: {{ agw_trace_sampling | default(0.1) }}
    # Do not let an incoming traceparent opt a request into tracing.
    clientSampling: false
    resources:
      service.name: '"agentgateway"'
    # Spans filter same-name defaults, then append CEL attributes (trc.rs:288-294,
    # 318-332); v1.5.0 exposes these under TracingConfig.attributes
    # (types/agent.rs:2452-2460).
    # https://github.com/agentgateway/agentgateway/blob/v1.5.0/crates/agentgateway/src/telemetry/trc.rs#L288-L294
    # https://github.com/agentgateway/agentgateway/blob/v1.5.0/crates/agentgateway/src/telemetry/trc.rs#L318-L332
    # https://github.com/agentgateway/agentgateway/blob/v1.5.0/crates/agentgateway/src/types/agent.rs#L2452-L2460
    attributes:
      http.path: request.path
{% endif %}
  http:
    # 20 MiB: Caddy caps request bodies at 16 MB; headroom so the edge, not the
    # gateway, is the component that answers 413.
    maxBufferSize: 20971520

llm:
  # Attached to BOTH gateways. On `default` (:4000) it is the API clients use. On
  # `ui` (:4001) it makes the UI's LLM Playground work: the UI calls /v1 on its
  # OWN origin when the LLM routes share its gateway (upstream ui/src/gatewayUrls.ts
  # `sameOrigin`), i.e. https://admin.inference.<zone>/v1 through Caddy — no CORS,
  # no second browser-reachable port. The OIDC policy above is scoped to the UI
  # routes: /v1 on :4001 is still apiKey-gated (401 without a key, verified
  # 2026-09-17), so the playground needs an enrolled client key pasted in.
{% if _loopback %}
  gateways: {{ '[default, ui, loopback]' if _ui else '[default, loopback]' }}
{% else %}
  gateways: {{ '[default, ui]' if _ui else '[default]' }}
{% endif %}
  policies:
{{ apikey_policy(agw_identities | default(agw_clients | default([])), true, true) | trim('\n') }}
    # Request-rate ceiling for the gateway as a whole. v1.5.0 has no per-identity
    # REQUEST bucket under the `llm` shortcut, verified against the binary
    # 2026-09-17: localRateLimit `key` is on main only (unreleased; the published
    # schema at agentgateway.dev tracks main) -> "unknown field `key`"; the
    # `conditional` form is accepted only on binds[].listeners[].routes[]
    # policies -> "expected a sequence". Per-identity control is the token
    # budget on each key above (operator's choice 2026-09-17: add the db rather
    # than wait for `key`); this ONE bucket protects the upstream's measured
    # concurrency ceiling. Revisit when a release ships `key`.
    localRateLimit:
      - type: requests
        maxTokens: {{ agw_rate_requests_per_minute_total | default(120) }}
        tokensPerFill: {{ agw_rate_requests_per_minute_total | default(120) }}
        fillInterval: 60s
  models:
{% for m in agw_models %}
{% if m.pool_identity is defined %}
  # Shared pool (above): the public name relays to this gateway's own loopback listener as the pool
  # identity, so the call is charged to the caller AND to the pool. The key is an env reference expanded
  # from the 0600 .env; this file holds no credential. Streamed chat asks for usage so the caller's own
  # budget is charged on this hop too.
  - name: {{ m.name }}
    provider:
      custom:
        formats:
        - type: completions
        - type: responses
    params:
      baseUrl: http://127.0.0.1:{{ _lb_port }}/v1
      model: {{ _pool_prefix ~ m.name }}
      apiKey: ${{ 'AGW_POOL_KEY_' ~ (m.pool_identity | upper | replace('-', '_')) }}
    transformation:
      stream_options: 'has(llmRequest.messages) && has(llmRequest.stream) && llmRequest.stream == true ? (has(llmRequest.stream_options) && type(llmRequest.stream_options) == map ? llmRequest.stream_options : {}).merge({"include_usage": true}) : llmRequest.stream_options'
{% endif %}
{% set _mname = (_pool_prefix ~ m.name) if m.pool_identity is defined else m.name %}
{% if (m.provider | default('')) == 'openrouter' %}
  # OpenRouter model (agentgateway-openrouter): the native `openrouter` provider is a custom-provider
  # preset in v1.5.0 that supplies the base URL https://openrouter.ai/api/v1 (HTTPS, so backend TLS
  # follows) and the OpenAI-compatible formats; crates/llm/src/custom.rs:52-67,81,144-153 and
  # crates/agentgateway/src/types/local.rs:872-875,1020-1022,1062-1064. `name` is what clients send,
  # params.model the OpenRouter id (vendor/model). The key is an env reference expanded by the
  # gateway from the 0600 .env; this file holds no credential. The deploy refuses a wildcard name
  # and an empty upstream_model, so exactly the models listed here are reachable, and the key
  # entries above allow them only to agw_openrouter_identities.
  - name: {{ _mname }}
    provider: openrouter
    params:
      model: {{ m.upstream_model }}
      apiKey: $OPENROUTER_API_KEY
    # Streamed chat is sent with include_usage so the per-key token budget charges it. Unlike the
    # vLLM models above there is no continuous_usage_stats: that field is a vLLM extension.
    # Measured 2026-10-15 through the gateway (agentgateway-openrouter task 3.5): OpenRouter accepts continuous_usage_stats (HTTP 200) but
    # still sends usage only on the final chunk, so adding it would change nothing; it is left out, so
    # usage arrives only on the final chunk and a client that disconnects before it is not charged. The
    # same CEL shape otherwise (chat only, a client's other stream_options keys kept).
    transformation:
      stream_options: 'has(llmRequest.messages) && has(llmRequest.stream) && llmRequest.stream == true ? (has(llmRequest.stream_options) && type(llmRequest.stream_options) == map ? llmRequest.stream_options : {}).merge({"include_usage": true}) : llmRequest.stream_options'
{% else %}
  # The gateway's model name equals the upstream's served name unless
  # `upstream_model` remaps it (e.g. a short client name for a longer served name).
  - name: {{ _mname }}
    provider:
      custom:
        # `responses` lets /v1/responses pass through to vLLM's own endpoint
        # (chat translation dropped reasoning items and echoed fields; prod
        # conformance task 2681). Format list, route pick and default path
        # `<baseUrl>/responses`: agentgateway v1.5.0 crates/llm/src/custom.rs:189-232,
        # crates/agentgateway/src/llm/mod.rs:324,991-1000,1338-1380.
        formats:
        - type: completions
        - type: responses
    params:
      baseUrl: {{ agw_upstream_base_url }}
{% if m.upstream_model | default('') or m.pool_identity is defined %}
      # A pooled entry is listed as pool/<name>; the upstream still receives the declared name.
      model: {{ m.upstream_model | default(m.name, true) }}
{% endif %}
{% if secrets.vllm_api_key | default('') %}
      apiKey: $VLLM_API_KEY
{% endif %}
    # Every streamed CHAT request is sent to vLLM with include_usage AND continuous_usage_stats
    # true, so the per-key token budget charges it (operator decision 2026-10-04, task 2.3a).
    # - The gateway adds stream_options itself only when the client sent none (agentgateway
    #   v1.5.0 crates/agentgateway/src/llm/mod.rs:1549-1560); without this a client sending
    #   {"include_usage": false} streamed for free.
    # - continuous_usage_stats: vLLM then puts cumulative usage on EVERY chunk (vllm
    #   entrypoints/serve/utils/api_utils.py should_include_usage). The gateway keeps the last
    #   usage it parsed (crates/llm/src/conversion/completions.rs:1207-1219) and settles the
    #   budget from it when the request ends (crates/agentgateway/src/telemetry/log.rs:1279-1280,
    #   http/budget/mod.rs:478-494 skips a response without usage). With only the final usage
    #   chunk, a client that disconnects after the answer is never charged. Clients therefore
    #   see a usage object on every chunk.
    # - Chat only: `has(llmRequest.messages)`; a Responses request (`input`) keeps its own
    #   stream_options untouched. A non-streamed request keeps what it sent; a client's other
    #   stream_options keys are kept (celx merge, later key wins: crates/celx/src/general.rs:303-313).
    # - Mechanism: per-model CEL body transformation (crates/agentgateway/src/types/local.rs:807-809,
    #   applied by crates/agentgateway/src/llm/policy/mod.rs:799-835; a CEL error removes the key).
    # Measured on the v1.5.0 image 2026-10-04: a stream dropped by the client after the answer is
    # charged with this, and not without it (platform/tests/test_agw_stream_usage.py).
    transformation:
      stream_options: 'has(llmRequest.messages) && has(llmRequest.stream) && llmRequest.stream == true ? (has(llmRequest.stream_options) && type(llmRequest.stream_options) == map ? llmRequest.stream_options : {}).merge({"include_usage": true, "continuous_usage_stats": true}) : llmRequest.stream_options'
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if _mcp %}
# MCP (agentgateway-mcp). One virtual MCP server at /mcp on the `default` gateway only (so /mcp is not also
# served on the operator-UI listener), authenticated by the SAME key policy as /v1 minus the identities that
# must never reach it: the legacy shared key, and mcp-self, which exists only as the target's own credential.
# It admits the admin-derived personal identities ONLY (_agw_admin_identities), never an agw_clients service
# client: the chat tool's self-call runs as mcp-self, so a service client admitted here would escape its own
# allowedModels and hourly budget. Admin MCP model spend is bounded by mcp-self's budget instead.
# - statefulMode stateless: no session id, no held server stream (so nothing sits against the 125 s edge
#   timeout), and GET /mcp is 405 (spike 1.6). The stateful session id is neither revocable nor bound to an
#   identity.
# - prefixMode conditional (the upstream default, pinned here so a change of default cannot rename tools): a
#   tool name is the OpenAPI operationId while there is one target, and is prefixed with the target name once
#   there are several.
# - No budgets under these keys: accepted, never charged (spike 1.4). The only budgeted path is mcp-self's.
# - No `timeout`, `retry` or dnsRebindingProtection: the gateway is reached by a non-local Host.
# Targets come only from the structured declaration agw_mcp_openapi_targets (name + schema file name); the
# deploy refuses anything else before this renders (vars/secret-declarations/agentgateway.yml,
# _agw_mcp_problems). The schema is the shipped file under deployment/mcp-schemas/, read by the deploy and
# embedded here as a string, so the config is self-contained (nothing to mount, and a schema change is a
# config change). backendAuth is the mcp-self key by environment reference; the value lives in the 0600 .env.
mcp:
  gateways: [default]
  statefulMode: stateless
  prefixMode: conditional
  targets:
{% for t in agw_mcp_openapi_targets | default([]) %}
  - name: {{ t.name }}
    openapi:
      schema: {{ _agw_mcp_schemas[t.schema] | to_json }}
      host: 127.0.0.1:{{ _lb_port }}
    policies:
      backendAuth:
        key:
          value: $AGW_MCP_SELF_KEY
          location:
            header:
              name: authorization
              prefix: 'Bearer '
{% endfor %}
  policies:
{{ apikey_policy((_agw_admin_identities | default([]) | map(attribute='name') | list) | reject('in', _internal_ids) | list, false, false) | trim('\n') }}
{% endif %}
{% set _skynet = agw_skynet_route_enabled | default(false) | bool %}
{% if _skynet %}
# The skynet orchestration route (agentgateway task 3.3, render half): `/skynet/...` on the default gateway,
# admitted only to the declared route identities. It has its own strict key policy, built by the same macro as
# /v1 minus the budgets and model lists (it is not an LLM surface), so a key the route does not name gets 401
# here and never reaches skynet. backendAuth.key is the env reference $SKYNET_API_TOKEN, which REPLACES the
# caller's Authorization on the upstream call, so skynet sees only its own bearer and never the caller's key
# (proven on the pinned v1.5.0 image 2026-10-19). No path rewrite: skynet serves under /skynet/ itself.
# Disabled, there is no `routes:` key at all, so a disabled render is what it was before this route existed.
routes:
- name: skynet
  # Pinned to the external listener: never the `ui` listener or the MCP self-call's `loopback` one
  # (`gateways` on a route is accepted by v1.5.0, validated 2026-10-19).
  gateways: [default]
  matches:
  - path: {pathPrefix: /skynet/}
  policies:
{{ apikey_policy(agw_skynet_route_identities | default([]), false, false) | trim('\n') }}
    backendAuth:
      key: $SKYNET_API_TOKEN
  backends:
  - host: {{ agw_skynet_backend }}
{% endif %}
